## Risks

- A heavier fault distribution may make `performance_lb 250` infeasible → G0-C catches it before any long run.
- Higher double-loss mass may drive `thruster_util` to binding (0.82–0.93 of budget already on six live) →
  the policy may under-use thrust for yaw; read the margin in G0-C and the yaw-rate exam in Phase 4; do
  NOT loosen the budget to compensate (`finding/052`).
- If m0/m3 are one motor (G0-G), keeping `My = 0.145` teaches a pitch moment the robot cannot make → the
  retrain would CREATE a gap; G0-G is therefore a hard prerequisite of the vertical-TAM decision.
- Training pitch through the arm may teach the policy to also use m0 (Fz/My coupling) → heave drift in
  the tank; the `thruster_util` constraint and `k_thr` penalty discourage it but do not forbid it; read
  `Fz` usage in the exam (`applied action` channel) before Phase 5.
- A DGX-trained teacher is a departure from the recorded caveat; mitigated by one-machine re-scoring and
  ≥2 seeds; residual risk is stated, not hidden.
- Student distillation is the measured bottleneck of the shipped artifact (every student 2.5–3.1° hard
  roll dispersion regardless of teacher); this program does not fix that and does not claim to.
- Orca on ksm-nas is not paired today; a local supervised worker over ssh loses nothing but adds one
  hop of failure (Mac must stay up).

## Predicted outcome

Most likely: G0-A shows the incumbent has NO arm-pitch fallback (θ2 span in sim mirrors the 5–7° seen
in the tank), G0-C clears with a paired deficit inside the seed gap, and the retrained teacher absorbs
the fault distribution — tracking pitch through the arm on the real-pair exam at `hard` with ss_error
within 2× of roll while roll stays inside the paired floor, and not collapsing on the single/double
losses the distribution covered. Delay DR `(0,1)` costs < 5 % return (the Z4 curve says d=1 is the
cheap step). Phase 0-R gives the rate a data answer; the tank then shows pitch steps with θ2 spans
≥ 15° — the first pitch tracking this robot has produced under RL.

Plausible null: pitch through a 2.2 N·m arm against a 6.10–7.76 N·m/rad restoring stiffness is
authority-limited (±14.5° at θ2 150° per T1), so a pitch step of 15° may settle near the envelope edge;
that would be a physics ceiling, not a training defect, and the paper-side story would shift to
"attitude within the arm envelope".

Failure mode to watch: the heavy fault distribution drops attainable return below `performance_lb` →
DORAEMON mode −2 from the start (the E1-latdr signature). Tell: `DORAEMON/success_rate` < 0.5 at
iteration 250 in BOTH seeds with the paired control above it.

